    "lawCode": "Amd Chaps 50, 53, 54 & 55 of 2008",
    "lawSection": "",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A163\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act to amend chapter 50 of the laws of 2008, enacting the public\nprotection and general government budget; to amend chapter 53 of the\nlaws of 2008, enacting the education, labor and family assistance budg-\net; to amend chapter 54 of the laws of 2008, enacting the health and\nmental hygiene budget; and to amend chapter 55 of the laws of 2008,\nenacting the transportation, economic development and environmental\nconservation budget, in relation to the support of state government\n\nPURPOSE:\nThis bill would amend the enacted 2008-09 Budget appropriation bills, as\namended this past August, to effect savings proposed by the Governor to\naddress the multi-year Financial Plan gap and respond to the worsening\nnational economic conditions faced by the State. Other bills being\nproposed in concert with this legislation would make non-appropriation\nstatutory changes needed to implement the proposed savings.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\nEffective immediately upon enactment, this bill makes the following\nchanges to appropriations highlighted as follows:\n\n* Reduces appropriations for Local Government Efficiency Grants and\nEfficiency Incentive Grants by amounts that will honor existing grant\nawards in full, but reduce funding for new grants by 50 percent and\nlimit Local Government Efficiency Grant administrative expenses to\n$500,000, generating savings of $1.7 million in 2008-09 and $13.6\nmillion in 2009-10;\n\n* Reduces 2008-09 funding for arts grants by $7 million;\n\n* Increases 2008-09 General Fund support by $12 million to accommodate\ngreater Tuition Assistance Program (TAP) spending that will result from\nState University of New York (SUNY) tuition rate increases;\n\n* Increases 2008-09 tuition revenue offset appropriations for SUNY by\n$76 million and reduces General Fund support for SUNY by $68 million,\nreflecting resident tuition rate increases of 14 percent;\n\n* Reduces 2008-09 General Fund support by $11 million for SUNY Community\nCollege base operating aid and $4 million for CUNY Community College\nbase operating aid;\n\n* Achieves over $70 million in savings in Medicaid and certain other\nHealth programs and offsetting HCRA revenues (other statutory changes\nneeded to produce this savings are proposed in a separate bill);\n\n* Achieves $1 million in 2008-09 savings by delaying implementation of\nslots for the foster care Bridges to Health Medicaid Waiver program\nuntil 2011-12;\n\n* Reduces appropriations for Mental Hygiene-related purposes by elimi-\nnating enriched Unified Services funding for certain counties and lower-\ning funding to the NYC Department of Education for prevention services\nthat are not predicated upon evidence-based practices;\n\n* Reduces 2008-09 funding for new and enhanced legislative programs by a\ncumulative total of 50 percent in 2008-09 and 2009-10;\n\n* Reduces the 2008-09 Human Services Cost of Living Adjustment (COLA) by\none percentage point for certain programs administered by the Department\nof Health, Office of Alcoholism and Substance Abuse Services, Office of\nChildren and Family Services, Office of Mental Health, Office of Mental\nRetardation and Developmental Disabilities, and State Office for the\nAging;\n\n* Reduces 2008-09 General Fund Local Assistance for various economic\ndevelopment initiatives by $8.5 million;\n\n* Reduces 2008-09 Environmental Protection Fund appropriations by $50\nmillion in various categories relating to Open Space, Solid Waste and\nParks and Recreation;\n\n* Achieves $2 million in 2008-09 savings by reducing appropriations for\nthe Neighborhood Preservation Program and the Rural Preservation\nProgram;\n\n* Achieves $3 million in 2008-09 savings by eliminating the operating\nsubsidy for the New York City Housing Authority;\n\n* Achieves $25 million in 2008-09 Financial Plan relief by creating\nthree new bonded capital appropriations for affordable housing programs\nthat were previously supported on a pay-as-you-go basis;\n\n* Shifts the funding for Timothy's Law and various Department of Health\nprograms from the General Fund to the Insurance Department's industry\nassessment;\n\n* Achieves $3 million in 2008-09 savings by eliminating a one-time\nappropriation from 2007-08 for diesel emissions reduction costs; and\n\n* Achieves $1.1 million in 2008-09 savings by eliminating a one-time\n2008-09 appropriation for multi-modal projects;\n\nSTATEMENT IN SUPPORT:\nThe Financial Plan savings resulting from this bill are part of the\npackage of savings options proposed to help close the gap and to help\nensure that the current year's budget remains in balance if economic\nconditions deteriorate further.\n\nBUDGETARY IMPLICATIONS:\nIn combination with other legislation that proposes statutory changes\nand fund transfers, this bill would produce the savings noted above.\n\nThese savings would substantially reduce the current 2008-09 and 2009-10\nprojected and beyond.\n\n",
